(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the fundamental principles and engineering applications of Surface Development, detailing why all lines drawn on a development must represent true lengths.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Foundation of Surface Development:) Tj
T*
(   - The development of a surface is the complete unfolding or unrolling of all bounding planar) Tj
T*
(   and curved surfaces of a three-dimensional hollow body onto a single flat two-dimensional) Tj
T*
(   plane.) Tj
T*
(   - When the developed 2D flat template is subsequently bent, folded, or rolled along prescribed) Tj
T*
(   fabrication lines and joined at its seams, it reconstructs the exact original 3D hollow object) Tj
T*
(   without geometric distortion.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The True Length Axiom:) Tj
T*
(   - FUNDAMENTAL METROLOGICAL LAW: Every single line segment, generator, boundary contour, and) Tj
T*
(   diagonal drawn on a surface development MUST REPRESENT THE TRUE PHYSICAL LENGTH of that feature) Tj
T*
(   in space!) Tj
T*
(   - Under no circumstances can a foreshortened apparent length \(from elevation or plan\) be) Tj
T*
(   transferred to a development.) Tj
T*
(   - If an apparent foreshortened length is used, the folded template will be too small, the) Tj
T*
(   angles will fail to close, and the component will be ruined during manufacturing.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Engineering Applications:) Tj
T*
(   - Sheet Metal Fabrication: Air conditioning HVAC ductwork, ventilation hoods, dust collectors,) Tj
T*
(   material transfer hoppers, chemical process piping, storage tanks, boiler pressure vessels.) Tj
T*
(   - Aerospace & Marine: Aircraft fuselage skins, wing transition fairings, ship hull plates.) Tj
T*
(   - Packaging Industry: Cardboard shipping cartons, sheet plastic containers, stamped tin cans.) Tj
T*
(   - Civil & Architectural: Tensile membrane fabric roofs, curved acoustic ceiling panels.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Classify developable and non-developable surfaces, explaining the four primary methods of development: Parallel Line, Radial Line, Triangulation, and Approximate methods.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mathematical Classification of Surfaces:) Tj
T*
(   - Developable Surfaces: Surfaces that possess zero Gaussian curvature \(K = 0\) at every point.) Tj
T*
(   They can be flattened into a plane without stretching, compressing, or tearing the material:) Tj
T*
(   * Polyhedral surfaces \(Prisms, Pyramids\).) Tj
T*
(   * Single-curved ruled surfaces \(Cylinders, Cones, Tangent developables\).) Tj
T*
(   - Non-Developable Surfaces: Doubly-curved surfaces that possess non-zero Gaussian curvature \(K) Tj
T*
(   != 0\). They cannot be flattened onto a plane without physical deformation:) Tj
T*
(   * Spheres, Ellipsoids, Paraboloids, Hyperbolic paraboloids, Toroids.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Four Primary Engineering Development Methods:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Parallel Line Development:) Tj
T*
(   * Employed for right regular prisms and cylinders where all longitudinal edges or surface) Tj
T*
(   generators are parallel to each other.) Tj
T*
(   * The pattern unfolds along a single continuous perpendicular stretch-out line \(girth line\).) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Radial Line Development:) Tj
T*
(   * Employed for right regular pyramids and right circular cones where all edges or generators) Tj
T*
(   radiate outward from a single apex point.) Tj
T*
(   * The pattern unfolds as a circular sector of radius equal to the true slant height.) Tj
T*
(   - 3. Triangulation Development:) Tj
T*
ET

