(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the procedure to design a sheet metal packaging carton with tuck-in flaps, glue tabs, and bend lines, detailing standard drafting line conventions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Architecture of a Packaging Carton Development:) Tj
T*
(   - A folding carton \(e.g. for software or consumer electronics\) is manufactured from a single) Tj
T*
(   flat sheet of bleached paperboard or corrugated fiberboard.) Tj
T*
(   - The development consists of four main body panels, top and bottom closure flaps, dust flaps,) Tj
T*
(   tuck-in tongues, and a side glue tab.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Line Conventions for Packaging Developments:) Tj
T*
(   - Outer Perimeter Cut Lines: Continuous thick lines \(Type A, 0.50 mm\) representing where the) Tj
T*
(   steel die-cutter physically cuts through the cardboard.) Tj
T*
(   - Internal Crease / Fold Lines: Dashed thin lines \(or red continuous thin lines, Type B, 0.25) Tj
T*
(   mm\) representing where the creasing rule scores the board for folding.) Tj
T*
(   - Perforations: Chain dashed lines for tear-away strips.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Step-by-Step Panel Layout \(Carton 80 x 50 x 120 mm\):) Tj
T*
(   - Main Body Stretch-Out:) Tj
T*
(   * Draw 4 body panels in series: Front \(80x120\), Side 1 \(50x120\), Back \(80x120\), Side 2) Tj
T*
(   \(50x120\). Total length = 260 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Glue Tab:) Tj
T*
(   * Attach a 15 mm wide glue flap along the outer edge of Side 2, beveled at 45? at both top and) Tj
T*
(   bottom corners.) Tj
T*
(   - Top and Bottom Closure Flaps:) Tj
T*
(   * Attach main tuck-in lid flaps \(80x50 mm\) to the top of Front and Back panels, terminating in) Tj
T*
(   a 15 mm rounded tuck-in tongue with friction lock slits.) Tj
T*
(   * Attach 35 mm dust flaps to the tops of Side 1 and Side 2 panels, angled at 15? to prevent) Tj
T*
(   jamming when closed.) Tj
T*
